content.text:
The DOM node is replaced on click, before focus, and log empty. Something calls renderAttributes during mousedown/click, replacing the input. What event is triggered before focus? **`mousedown`** maybe triggers `updateFromForm`? No handlers. The `#content-name` blur? Blur happens before focus; if the input is replaced on mousedown by an event handler that calls `syncInputs`/`renderAttributes`. Which global handler runs on mousedown? None. Wait, could **the browser dispatch a `change` event on the content-name input when it loses focus**? The content-name had a value set via Playwright fill and perhaps not blurred; when clicking another input, `change` fires on content-name. The app has a `change` listener on `#content-name` (`addEventListener('change', updateFromForm)`). Yes! In init:
```
['content-name', ...].forEach((id) => {
  addEventListener('input', updateFromForm);
  addEventListener('change', updateFromForm);
});
```
When content-name loses focus, its `change` event fires, calling `updateFromForm()`, which now calls `renderAttributes()` and replaces the input under the cursor. That's why focus fails. This is a real UX bug: any time you type in name and then click an attribute field, the field gets replaced before focus, so you can't edit it! Similarly changing the name and then clicking other fields could replace attributes only.

Even without Playwright, a user types name, then clicks damage; blur triggers `change` → updateFromForm → renderAttributes → input replaced → click lost. That's serious.

Solution: Don't re-render attributes on every `updateFromForm` if definitions haven't changed, or preserve focus. Options:
1. In updateFromForm, only call `renderAttributes()` if the attribute definitions set changed (type/kind changed). Currently it calls every time. Compute a signature:
```
let renderedAttributeSignature = '';
function renderAttributes(force=false) {
  const defs = attributeDefinitions();
  const signature = state.type + '|' + state.kind + '|' + defs.map(d=>d.key).join(',');
  if (!force && signature === renderedAttributeSignature) {
     // update values? maybe not
     return;
  }
  renderedAttributeSignature = signature;
  ... render ...
}
```
But if values changed externally (AI merge/template), we need re-render even if signature same. `syncInputs` calls renderAttributes and should force. Add `renderAttributes(force = false)`; in updateFromForm call `renderAttributes(false)`; in syncInputs call `renderAttributes(true)`. On input event attribute, no render. On type/kind change, signature changes, so it re-renders (even from updateFromForm); click on attribute after name change with same kind won't re-render, preserving focus. If the name changes kind (e.g., adding "espada" makes sword), signature changes on input; the attribute fields re-render while typing in name, but the user is focused on name, so fine. When clicking an attribute, if kind changes due to the name value, updateFromForm on change will see same signature (because it already re-rendered during input), so no replace. Good.
- `renderAttributes(true)` in syncInputs after template/type changes. Actually type switch calls syncInputs; signature different, render anyway. Force updates values if same structure but state values changed (e.g., template loads weapon while already weapon: update fields values). In useTemplate, syncInputs → force true, so values updated.
- AI merge → syncInputs force true.
- loadState → syncInputs force true.
- updateFromForm after attribute input? not called.
- `renderAttributes` called directly in updateFromForm with false.

2. Alternatively call renderAttributes on `focus`? no.

Implement signature. Also, `renderAttributes` is called in syncInputs after hint, with force true. Ensure signature is per defs + type/kind.

But another issue: **Clicking attribute input when it is not focused and content-name `change` triggers updateFromForm** — with signature same, no replace. Good.

Also, **clicking the attribute field when kind changes due to name blur**: if the user typed a new name that changes kind, updateFromForm during input already re-rendered; signature up-to-date. Good.

Let's patch.

